@page "/blogs/distributed-testing-with-aspire-and-playwright-in-dotnet"
@using SharedModels
@using BaseComponents
@inherits BasePage

<Content FileName=@nameof(DistributedTestingWithAspireAndPlaywrightInDotNet)>
    <ContentBody>
        <What>
            <p>
                A green test can still leave the most important question unanswered: did your API,
                database, and UI work together? <Highlight>Aspire</Highlight> can start the real
                application resources for a test, while <Highlight>Playwright</Highlight> drives a
                real browser against the running UI.
            </p>
            <p>
                This walkthrough extends the
                <a class="underline" href="/blogs/getting-started-with-aspire-in-dotnet/">Aspire catalog sample</a>.
                For copy-and-run steps, create that article's optional reader-built sample first;
                if you wired an existing application instead, substitute its project, resource,
                and endpoint names. You will start a disposable AppHost, submit through the UI,
                reload the page, and verify the seeded and newly saved items are still rendered.
                An API round-trip is included as a narrower, optional check.
            </p>
        </What>

        <Why>
            <p>
                API functional tests and browser tests answer different questions. A functional
                test can use <Highlight>WebApplicationFactory</Highlight> to send in-process
                requests through an API pipeline, and it can use a real database if configured;
                it is usually faster and isolates one service boundary.
            </p>
            <p>
                An Aspire-backed browser test starts separate service processes and exercises the
                real UI, service discovery, endpoint settings, and database connection together.
                It catches integration failures that an in-process test may not cover, but startup,
                containers, and a real browser make it slower and more operationally demanding.
                Neither approach replaces the other, and a test that uses a fake dependency should
                say so rather than imply full-system coverage.
            </p>
            <p>
                A process being <Highlight>running</Highlight> only means it started; a resource
                becoming <Highlight>healthy</Highlight> means its health check passed; and being
                <Highlight>business-ready</Highlight> means the operation you care about succeeds.
                The walkthrough keeps a fast API round-trip as a lower-level companion, then uses
                Playwright to submit through the UI and prove the saved item is still visible after
                a browser reload.
            </p>
        </Why>

        <How>
            <h4>1. Reference the AppHost from a dedicated distributed test project</h4>
            <p>
                Complete the preceding catalog walkthrough first, then stop its AppHost. Allow
                roughly 30–60 minutes plus browser and image downloads. Work from the
                <Highlight>Store</Highlight> directory, with the container runtime running.
                Create the <Highlight>Store.Tests</Highlight> directory and
                <Highlight>Store.Tests/Store.Tests.csproj</Highlight> beside the AppHost with
                the following content. Its AppHost reference lets Aspire discover the generated
                project type. Keep package versions aligned with the .NET and Aspire versions
                used by the sample. Install PowerShell 7 if <Highlight>pwsh</Highlight> is not
                already available; the generated <Highlight>playwright.ps1</Highlight> script
                uses it on Windows, macOS, and Linux.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-xml">
&lt;Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk"&gt;
    &lt;PropertyGroup&gt;
        &lt;TargetFramework&gt;net11.0&lt;/TargetFramework&gt;
        &lt;OutputType&gt;Exe&lt;/OutputType&gt;
        &lt;IsPackable&gt;false&lt;/IsPackable&gt;
        &lt;IsTestProject&gt;true&lt;/IsTestProject&gt;
        &lt;Nullable&gt;enable&lt;/Nullable&gt;
        &lt;ImplicitUsings&gt;enable&lt;/ImplicitUsings&gt;
    &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;

    &lt;ItemGroup&gt;
        &lt;PackageReference Include="Aspire.Hosting.Testing" Version="13.6.5" /&gt;
        &lt;PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="18.12.0" /&gt;
        &lt;PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Playwright.Xunit.v3" Version="1.64.1" /&gt;
        &lt;PackageReference Include="xunit.v3" Version="4.0.3" /&gt;
        &lt;PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="4.0.1"&gt;
            &lt;PrivateAssets&gt;all&lt;/PrivateAssets&gt;
            &lt;IncludeAssets&gt;runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive&lt;/IncludeAssets&gt;
        &lt;/PackageReference&gt;
    &lt;/ItemGroup&gt;

    &lt;ItemGroup&gt;
        &lt;ProjectReference Include="..\Store.AppHost\Store.AppHost.csproj" /&gt;
    &lt;/ItemGroup&gt;
&lt;/Project&gt;
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Replace the relative project path if your test project lives elsewhere. The
                <Highlight>Aspire.Hosting.Testing</Highlight> package supplies the test host APIs;
                <Highlight>Microsoft.Playwright.Xunit.v3</Highlight> supplies the browser test base
                for xUnit v3. Set the test project's <Highlight>TargetFramework</Highlight> to
                match the AppHost's target framework; this sample uses <Highlight>net11.0</Highlight>.
                Pin compatible package versions in your repository so updates stay deliberate.
            </p>

            <h4>2. Start one isolated AppHost with bounded startup and safe cleanup</h4>
            <p>
                Copy all three blocks below in order into one file,
                <Highlight>Store.Tests/AspireFixture.cs</Highlight>. Part 1 declares the fixture
                and starts its AppHost.
                Use an assembly fixture when multiple tests can share one expensive application
                startup. The linked cancellation token bounds container pulls, resource startup, and
                readiness checks; disposing the AppHost tears down its ephemeral resources.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using Aspire.Hosting;
using Aspire.Hosting.Testing;
using Xunit;

[assembly: AssemblyFixture(typeof(AspireFixture))]

public sealed class AspireFixture : IAsyncLifetime
{
    private static readonly TimeSpan StartupLimit = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(120);
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _shutdown = new();
    private IDistributedApplicationTestingBuilder? _builder;
    private DistributedApplication? _app;
    private HttpClient? _apiClient;

    public HttpClient ApiClient =&gt;
        _apiClient ?? throw new InvalidOperationException("The AppHost has not started.");

    public Uri WebUrl { get; private set; } = null!;

    public async ValueTask InitializeAsync()
    {
        using var startup = CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource(
            _shutdown.Token, TestContext.Current.CancellationToken);
        startup.CancelAfter(StartupLimit);

        try
        {
            _builder = await DistributedApplicationTestingBuilder
                .CreateAsync&lt;Projects.Store_AppHost&gt;(startup.Token);
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>Part 2 builds and starts the AppHost, waits for health, and checks HTTP operations:</p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
            _app = await _builder.BuildAsync(startup.Token);
            await _app.StartAsync(startup.Token);
            await _app.ResourceNotifications.WaitForResourceHealthyAsync(
                "catalog-api",
                startup.Token);
            await _app.ResourceNotifications.WaitForResourceHealthyAsync(
                "store-web",
                startup.Token);

            _apiClient = _app.CreateHttpClient("catalog-api");
            WebUrl = _app.GetEndpoint("store-web", "https");

            using var apiResponse = await ApiClient.GetAsync(
                "/products",
                startup.Token);
            apiResponse.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();

            using var webClient = new HttpClient();
            using var webResponse = await webClient.GetAsync(
                WebUrl,
                startup.Token);
            webResponse.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        }
        catch
        {
            await DisposeAsync();
            throw;
        }
    }
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>Part 3 finishes the same class and cleans up its resources:</p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
    private bool _disposed;

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        if (_disposed)
        {
            return;
        }
        _disposed = true;
        _shutdown.Cancel();
        try
        {
            _apiClient?.Dispose();
            if (_app is not null)
            {
                await _app.DisposeAsync();
            }
            else if (_builder is not null)
            {
                await _builder.DisposeAsync();
            }
        }
        finally
        {
            _shutdown.Dispose();
        }
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The resource notifications wait for the AppHost health checks; the
                <Highlight>GET /products</Highlight> request then checks an API operation, and the
                web request checks that its endpoint responds. The fixture does not assume that a
                running container is ready, and it does not use a fixed port or an unbounded
                <Highlight>Task.Delay</Highlight>. The testing builder randomizes proxied resource
                ports by default; explicitly configured non-proxied ports can still conflict.
                The startup token also observes xUnit's test-run cancellation. Cleanup is
                idempotent if startup fails and the runner subsequently disposes the fixture.
            </p>
            <p>
                <Highlight>AssemblyFixture</Highlight> makes xUnit create this
                <Highlight>AspireFixture</Highlight> once for the test assembly and inject it into
                test-class constructors. Both the API test and the Playwright test below receive
                the same running AppHost and <Highlight>ApiClient</Highlight>; the latter derives
                from <Highlight>PageTest</Highlight>, which still gives each test its own browser
                context. Use an xUnit collection fixture instead if only a group of related test
                classes should share an application.
            </p>
            <p>
                Keep the AppHost database ephemeral and omit a persistent volume for this test
                environment, so each new fixture run starts with the same seeded baseline from the
                API startup step. The fixture shares that database for the whole test assembly; it
                does not reset state between test methods. Unique records keep concurrent tests
                independent for these append-only assertions. An assembly fixture does not
                serialize test classes; tests that count, delete, or mutate shared data need
                stronger isolation or explicit synchronization.
            </p>
            <p>
                At normal suite teardown, xUnit calls the fixture's asynchronous disposal, which
                stops the AppHost and removes its managed test resources. This disposable local
                host keeps test rows out of shared staging databases. Cleanup is not guaranteed if
                the process or machine is forcibly terminated, and it cannot remove data from a
                persistent volume or external database; point the test graph only at disposable
                resources. Think start-use-dispose, not an unawaited background task or a promise
                that cleanup survives a machine crash.
            </p>
            <p>
                The AppHost must expose the same <Highlight>/health</Highlight> checks configured
                for <Highlight>catalog-api</Highlight> and <Highlight>store-web</Highlight> in the
                getting-started article. ServiceDefaults commonly exposes health endpoints in the
                Development environment; keep the test launch configuration consistent with that
                setup. Health means infrastructure readiness, not proof that every business workflow
                works.
            </p>

            <h4>3. Add an optional API check for a narrower failure signal</h4>
            <p>
                Copy both blocks below in order into one file,
                <Highlight>Store.Tests/CatalogApiTests.cs</Highlight>.
                Give every test its own product name so parallel tests do not depend on execution
                order or overwrite shared records. This focused test posts a product, reads the
                catalog again, and checks its generated identifier and value. It complements the
                browser journey; it does not replace the user-visible assertion below.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using System.Net;
using System.Net.Http.Json;
using Xunit;

public sealed class CatalogApiTests(AspireFixture fixture)
{
    [Fact]
    public async Task Post_product_then_read_it_from_catalog()
    {
        var name = $"api-{Guid.NewGuid():N}";
        var ct = TestContext.Current.CancellationToken;

        using var postResponse = await fixture.ApiClient.PostAsJsonAsync(
            "/products",
            new { name }, ct);
        Assert.Equal(HttpStatusCode.OK, postResponse.StatusCode);

        var created = await postResponse.Content.ReadFromJsonAsync&lt;Product&gt;(ct);
        Assert.NotNull(created);
        Assert.Equal(name, created.Name);
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>Continue the same method to read and compare the created record:</p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
        using var getResponse = await fixture.ApiClient.GetAsync("/products", ct);
        getResponse.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        var products = await getResponse.Content.ReadFromJsonAsync&lt;Product[]&gt;(ct);

        Assert.NotNull(products);
        Assert.Contains(products, product =&gt;
            product.Id == created.Id &amp;&amp; product.Name == name);
    }

    public sealed record Product(Guid Id, string Name);
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The API inserts the record into PostgreSQL and the subsequent request retrieves it
                through the API. Comparing the same identifier and name makes this stronger than
                checking only for an HTTP success code, without coupling the test to database
                internals or credentials.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-text">
Optional API-level assertion:
    test client --POST /products--&gt; catalog-api --&gt; catalog-db
    test client --GET  /products--&gt; catalog-api --&gt; catalog-db

Browser journey:
    Playwright submits form --&gt; store-web --&gt; catalog-api --&gt; catalog-db
    Browser reloads /catalog --&gt; store-web --&gt; catalog-api --&gt; catalog-db
    Rendered page shows the persisted product
            </CodeSnippet>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-text">
Fresh ephemeral host:
    id                                    name
    ------------------------------------  -------------------------
    11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111  Sample notebook
    22222222-2222-4222-8222-222222222222  Sample pen

After browser submission and reload:
    id                                    name
    ------------------------------------  -------------------------
    the same two seeded IDs               seed names still visible
    generated for this test               browser-&lt;unique-guid&gt;
            </CodeSnippet>

            <h4>4. Submit, revisit, and verify the saved product in the browser</h4>
            <p>
                Save this snippet as <Highlight>Store.Tests/CatalogBrowserTests.cs</Highlight>.
                Use the actual HTTPS endpoint allocated by the AppHost, not a remembered localhost
                port. Copy both blocks in order into that file. The test observes the seeded
                baseline, submits a uniquely named item through the accessible form, and verifies
                that the UI still renders it after a fresh page request.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using Microsoft.Playwright;
using Microsoft.Playwright.Xunit.v3;
using Xunit;

public sealed class CatalogBrowserTests(AspireFixture fixture) : PageTest
{
    [Fact]
    public async Task Browser_form_saves_a_product_to_the_catalog()
    {
        var name = $"browser-{Guid.NewGuid():N}";

        var catalogUrl = new Uri(fixture.WebUrl, "/catalog");
        await Page.GotoAsync(catalogUrl.ToString());
        var productList = Page.GetByRole(
            AriaRole.List,
            new() { Name = "Products" });
        await Expect(productList.GetByText("Sample notebook")).ToBeVisibleAsync();
        await Expect(productList.GetByText("Sample pen")).ToBeVisibleAsync();

        await Page.GetByLabel("Product name").FillAsync(name);
        await Page.GetByRole(
            AriaRole.Button,
            new() { Name = "Add product" }).ClickAsync();

        await Expect(Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Status))
            .ToHaveTextAsync($"Added {name}");
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>Part 2 requests the catalog again and verifies the persisted item is still rendered:</p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
        await Page.GotoAsync(catalogUrl.ToString());
        await Expect(productList.GetByText("Sample notebook")).ToBeVisibleAsync();
        await Expect(productList.GetByText("Sample pen")).ToBeVisibleAsync();
        await Expect(productList.GetByText(name)).ToBeVisibleAsync();
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                <Highlight>PageTest</Highlight> manages the Playwright browser and page for the
                test. Role-based locators follow the accessible page structure, and Playwright
                assertions auto-wait for the expected element instead of racing a fixed sleep.
                The submitted form makes the web service call the API and PostgreSQL; a fresh page
                request makes the UI fetch the list again. Seeing both the seeded item and this
                test's unique item after that request proves the full browser-to-web-to-API-to-
                database-to-rendered-UI round trip. The separate API test remains useful for
                precise service-level failures, but it is not the proof of this user-facing flow.
            </p>

            <h4>5. Run the tests locally</h4>
            <p>
                Start the container runtime and build the test project before installing its
                matching Playwright browser. On Windows and macOS, trust the local HTTPS
                development certificate with the first command. Keep <Highlight>$tfm</Highlight>
                aligned with the test project's target framework (and the AppHost's target
                framework); these commands run from <Highlight>Store</Highlight>:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-powershell">
dotnet dev-certs https --trust
dotnet build .\Store.Tests\Store.Tests.csproj
$tfm = "net11.0"
pwsh ".\Store.Tests\bin\Debug\$tfm\playwright.ps1" install chromium
dotnet test .\Store.Tests\Store.Tests.csproj --no-build
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The first command is for Windows and macOS. On Linux, don't rely on
                <Highlight>dotnet dev-certs https --trust</Highlight>; follow the distribution-
                and browser-specific certificate steps in Microsoft's
                <a class="underline" href="https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/security/enforcing-ssl#trust-the-aspnet-core-https-development-certificate">HTTPS development-certificate guide</a>;
                both the fixture's .NET HTTP client and Playwright's browser must trust the
                certificate. Don't bypass certificate checks to make the test pass.
            </p>
            <p>
                A successful run discovers and passes two tests: the API round-trip and the
                browser catalog assertion. If none are discovered, check the xUnit v3 runner and
                project reference. If startup times out, inspect container-runtime access and
                resource logs; if an HTTPS request or navigation fails, check the actual endpoint
                and the trust stores used by both clients. Playwright's browser install downloads
                local browser binaries; refresh them after changing the Playwright package version.
            </p>
            <p>
                Keep local test data synthetic and ephemeral, and never put production credentials
                or connection strings in source control. Do not retry the whole test to hide a
                readiness or contract failure.
            </p>

            <h4>6. Orchestrate multiple repositories without pretending Aspire checks them out</h4>
            <p>
                Aspire models resources; your source-control or image pipeline obtains their code.
                For project references, have your build pipeline check out the API, worker, and web
                repositories at controlled commits into a known workspace, then let the AppHost
                reference those projects. Alternatively, publish versioned container images and
                have the AppHost start pinned image tags. In both cases, keep endpoints reachable
                from the test machine and use ephemeral databases rather than production data or
                credentials.
            </p>
            <p>
                A multi-repository test is meaningful only when it verifies a contract across those
                repositories. Keep the UI-submit, reload, and UI-verification flow above as the user-facing boundary;
                add a separate assertion for another service only when that service's observable
                behavior matters to the contract.
            </p>
            <p>
                The AppHost also gives <Highlight>AI coding agents</Highlight> a cross-repository
                system map. When an agent has access to the relevant code and runtime diagnostics,
                it can connect a failing browser step to downstream services, inspect their logs
                and traces, and rerun the complete workflow after a fix. That is stronger context
                than changing one repository without knowing the distributed system it belongs to;
                the browser assertions remain the evidence that the user journey works.
            </p>

            <h4>7. Extend the graph to Kafka only when you assert message delivery</h4>
            <p>
                The following is an optional AppHost sketch, not part of the runnable catalog
                sample. It starts a Kafka resource and gives a worker its connection information;
                it does not configure a consumer or prove that a message was delivered.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
var events = builder.AddKafka("events");

builder.AddProject&lt;Projects.Store_Worker&gt;("catalog-worker")
    .WithReference(events)
    .WaitFor(events);
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                To test messaging, publish a uniquely identified event and assert an observable
                consumer result, such as a persisted status or a documented API response. Waiting
                for the Kafka broker to become healthy proves only that the broker passed its health
                check. Use a bounded, event-based or condition-polling assertion for the consumer
                result; do not add an arbitrary sleep or retry the entire test to hide delivery bugs.
            </p>

